WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.376 --> 00:00:03.044
These are the blooms of prune trees.

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:07.757
Located in a geologic trough, the 
Santa Clara Valley in California

00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:10.510
was once known as the 
Valley of Heart's Delight

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:15.098
due to its farmland filled with 
flowers and fruit, including prunes.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:19.060
How did a region known for 
its fruit and flowers...

00:00:19.644 --> 00:00:22.439
become an intellectual capital?

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:30.447
During World War II, 
American engineer

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:33.700
Vannevar Bush basically led 
wartime R&amp;D.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.369
And in 1945

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:39.539
he published an essay called 
“Science: The Endless Frontier”.

00:00:39.748 --> 00:00:42.083
Bush reported to President Roosevelt that

00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:45.336
the pioneer spirit is still vigorous
within this nation.

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:48.757
Though focused partly on problems
like the war against disease...

00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:51.551
the report also set an expectation...

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:54.846
that science and continuing science funding...

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:57.307
was necessary for the public welfare.

00:00:57.724 --> 00:01:01.061
Just a few years later, the National 
Science Foundation resulted

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:03.271
as Harry Truman wrote, Henry Ford

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:06.733
to promote industrial development 
in national defense.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.694
This funding meant facilities

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:12.072
like NASA's Ames then called NACA...

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:15.909
and Navy stations near San Francisco, 
provided a continuing

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:19.454
engineering talent base to 
the San Francisco Bay Area.

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.832
That was paired with proximity 
to Stanford University...

00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:26.503
the college that had greater 
land resources than cash.

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:30.381
Built from the land grant 
of Leland Stanford

00:01:30.381 --> 00:01:32.175
the university was land rich.

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:34.511
You can see in this early proposal

00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:37.555
how a small campus was 
surrounded by farmland.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:40.016
But Stanford didn't have 
a ton of cash.

00:01:40.308 --> 00:01:43.103
The region had a lot of unrealized potential...

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.939
and one of Vannevar Bush's 
former students...

00:01:46.314 --> 00:01:47.565
wanted to realize it.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.779
Frederick Terman was a professor of 
electrical engineering at Stanford

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.490
as well as an iconic name in radio science.

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:58.827
He worked on stuff like jamming Nazi radar
during World War II

00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:01.496
and he also wrote a radio engineering textbook

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:03.790
that sold hundreds of thousands of copies.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:08.294
Even before Terman was appointed 
university provost in 1955

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:11.381
as a professor and as a dean, 
he sought ways to develop 

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:12.966
the Valley of Heart's Delight.

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:17.679
This 1950s land use study

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.431
shows that Stanford intended 
to use some of its land

00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:23.434
primarily for the campus 
and residential development.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:26.312
Stanford developed the 
Stanford Shopping Mall

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.106
to help remedy their lack of cash.

00:02:28.356 --> 00:02:30.483
It's still leased by the university today.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.902
This is their California Pizza Kitchen.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:34.904
More importantly...

00:02:35.113 --> 00:02:38.908
Terman helped the university develop 
Stanford Industrial Park...

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:40.994
today called Stanford Research Park.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.872
This is what it looked like in 1953.

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:48.084
The park provided affordable 
99 year leases to tenants

00:02:48.084 --> 00:02:49.586
with a strict development plan

00:02:49.586 --> 00:02:52.463
to preserve the character of 
Palo Alto Construction.

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:56.301
They insisted on low buildings 
and preserved grassland...

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:58.803
that the Valley of Heart's Delight 
was known for.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.431
But it was more than just a development

00:03:01.431 --> 00:03:02.765
on the university's land.

00:03:02.891 --> 00:03:05.935
From the beginning, 
Terman imagined a new cycle.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:09.981
Stanford to companies... 
to Stanford to companies...

00:03:09.981 --> 00:03:13.693
[repeating and overlapping]
to Standford to companies...

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:18.031
As he said in one interview for 
Palo Alto 75th anniversary...

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:20.617
“And I would say university made 
a major contribution...

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.160
to the development here.”

00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:24.454
“But then the companies made 
a major contribution

00:03:24.454 --> 00:03:26.873
to the development of those parts 
of the university

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.208
that contributed to these companies.”

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:30.710
He encouraged this in many ways

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.837
including allowing professors

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:36.007
to spend time in corporate roles 
and get corporate paydays...

00:03:36.257 --> 00:03:40.428
and helping companies enroll employees 
as Stanford students.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.096
He proudly clipped this article

00:03:42.096 --> 00:03:44.891
about faculty eggheads 
becoming millionaires.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:49.062
And he gave speeches about how 
Stanford is also a source

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.522
of highly trained manpower 
for those companies...

00:03:51.773 --> 00:03:55.318
at a time when such manpower 
is in short supply.

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:58.363
Crucially, this cycle wasn't predicated

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:01.449
on silicon or semiconductor companies.

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:03.368
Stanford Industrial Park was open

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:05.828
for any tenant that wanted to be 
near Stanford.

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:08.248
Houghton Mifflin, 
the book publisher

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:10.959
staked to claim in the Stanford 
Industrial Park.

00:04:11.292 --> 00:04:14.003
Other hi-tech but not computer-focused companies

00:04:14.003 --> 00:04:17.590
also found spots there, as shown 
in this 1960 picture.

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:21.344
Here's Terman at the announcement 
of a Stanford Research Park building

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:23.721
for SpinCo, a maker of centrifuges

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:27.642
and division of a larger company
looking to expand in Palo Alto.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.353
Stanford's landholdings quickly changed.

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:33.189
You can see how it expanded 
through 1955

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:38.236
with 120 acres into 
350 acres by 1960. 

00:04:38.236 --> 00:04:41.739
Terman explicitly said this cycle 
would not only affect the Bay Area

00:04:41.739 --> 00:04:43.491
but the national labor market.

00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:47.870
It worked and in electronics focus 
grew from Terman’s own interest

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:48.788
in engineering.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:52.709
West Coast electronic firms 
produced 22% 

00:04:52.709 --> 00:04:56.337
of the US’s electronic market in 1960, 
and it kept growing.

00:04:56.421 --> 00:04:59.048
This was a competition between regions.

00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:03.094
Terman said if the Midwest continues 
to plod along in electronics...

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:05.722
it is destined to become the peon group...

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:08.308
in the nation's electronics industry.

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:11.936
He also argued that growth industries 
depend on brains...

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:16.107
and the best source of brains is 
the nation's major universities.

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:20.653
Proximity of markets no longer 
will play a key role.

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:25.116
This is the Palo Alto garage where 
Hewlett-Packard was founded.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:26.868
Terman had been a teacher.

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.748
And his personal solicitations helped

00:05:31.748 --> 00:05:33.166
bring them to the West Coast.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:36.961
And later, a short drive away 
in Stanford Industrial Park...

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:40.006
where HP secured a 40-acre site.

00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:42.675
Stanford Industrial Park became a platform

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.219
for any successful industry that might emerge.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.681
William Shockley's Shockley 
Semiconductor Laboratory 

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:52.685
is widely credited with starting 
the Silicon semiconductor boom...

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.146
and Fred Terman solicited Shockley

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:57.565
to start his business
in the Valley.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.650
He did so in nearby Mountain View.

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:01.235
Shockley Semiconductors

00:06:01.235 --> 00:06:05.073
became a demonstration of the final cycle
that Terman initiated:

00:06:05.615 --> 00:06:07.784
Engineers in the ecosystem

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:10.536
would found new companies 
in the same area.

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:13.081
Employees split off Shockley

00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:15.792
to form Fairchild Semiconductors 
in Mountain View...

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.004
and then those employees split off 
to form Intel locally...

00:06:20.171 --> 00:06:21.964
and so on and so on.

00:06:23.758 --> 00:06:24.967
This paired with development

00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:26.803
of more recognizable companies

00:06:26.803 --> 00:06:28.638
that started in the industrial park.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:31.432
Renamed the Research Park in 1970.

00:06:31.682 --> 00:06:35.937
Xerox's Palo Alto reach center, 
PARC was located there.

00:06:36.187 --> 00:06:38.439
“This is an experimental office system.”

00:06:38.648 --> 00:06:40.983
“It's in use now at the 
Xerox research center

00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:42.276
in Palo Alto, California.”

00:06:42.276 --> 00:06:44.612
As venture capital grew in the 1960s...

00:06:44.946 --> 00:06:47.865
bearby Sandhill Road was 
an obvious location

00:06:47.865 --> 00:06:50.660
due to its proximity 
to the Stanford epicenter.

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:53.413
By 1971, journalist Don Hoefler

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:56.249
former publicist for Fairchild Semiconductor

00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:59.877
labeled the region Silicon Valley 
for a series of articles.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:02.088
It was an allusion to the silicon chips

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.257
that had taken over the valley.

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:05.174
As Hoffler wrote...

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:08.052
The pace has been so frantic that even hardened

00:07:08.052 --> 00:07:10.179
veterans of the semiconductor wars...

00:07:10.471 --> 00:07:15.643
find it hard to realize that the Bay Area story 
covers an era of only 15 years.

00:07:16.561 --> 00:07:18.229
The Valley became home to Oracle...

00:07:18.521 --> 00:07:21.607
and Next and Adobe 
and Sun Microsystems.

00:07:21.899 --> 00:07:23.943
And from Netscape on 
powered forward...

00:07:24.235 --> 00:07:25.903
in the first dotcom boom.

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:28.072
“In 1994 and 95...

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:31.659
Netscape was known as the fastest growing 
company in the industry...

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:34.245
with all the requisite valley attributes.”

00:07:34.579 --> 00:07:36.831
And even in a remote work era:

00:07:36.831 --> 00:07:41.919
Meta, Apple, and Google have all 
maintained Silicon Valley...

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.380
as a substantial hub.

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:47.049
Terman’s vision made that cycle possible.

00:07:47.717 --> 00:07:50.136
And today's Silicon Valley 
has fewer prunes...

00:07:50.428 --> 00:07:53.556
but it remains in all its complicated ways...

00:07:54.265 --> 00:07:56.184
a Valley of Heart's Delight.

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:01.314
Hey, thanks for watching this history

00:08:01.314 --> 00:08:05.151
of how Silicon Valley became 
Silicon Valley.

00:08:05.443 --> 00:08:06.152
But I've got—

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:08.029
One more thing.

00:08:08.112 --> 00:08:11.199
Is that corny? No? Should I have
not done that? Anyway. 

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:14.035
The map that I use of Stanford Research Park...

00:08:14.243 --> 00:08:16.537
It goes up to a certain point in the video...

00:08:16.621 --> 00:08:19.207
but it actually comes from a historical survey...

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:20.666
that continues in time.

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:22.627
So I want to show the rest of that to you here.

00:08:22.627 --> 00:08:25.755
By 1965, it is up to 500 acres.

00:08:25.796 --> 00:08:27.089
That's the yellow area.

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:29.258
The light green area shows you

00:08:29.258 --> 00:08:33.638
that it's ballooned to 575 acres by 1970.

00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:36.641
By 75, it's at 600 acres.

00:08:36.641 --> 00:08:37.850
That's the dark green.

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:41.604
And then you've got the blue, 
which shows you up to 1980

00:08:41.604 --> 00:08:46.275
which expands Stanford Research Park 
to 700 acres.

